.Dd August 21, 2026
.Dt XO_PLUGINS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xo_plugins
.Nd LLVM/clang plugins for libxo
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm libxo
ships two optional LLVM/clang plugins that operate on
.Fn xo_emit
family calls at compile time:
.Bl -tag -width "xo_precompile"
.It Sy xo_validate
a clang AST plugin that checks
.Nm libxo
format strings for syntax errors and checks each format string's
field descriptors against the types and count of the arguments
actually passed, much like
.Fl Wformat
does for
.Fn printf .
.It Sy xo_precompile
an LLVM IR pass that finds
.Nm libxo
calls whose format string is a compile-time constant, parses that
format string during the build, and rewrites the call to
.Fn xo_emit_cached ,
passing a pre-parsed field table as a constant.
This removes the runtime cost of parsing the format string on every
call.
.El
.Pp
Both plugins share a single format-string parser (the same one
.Nm libxo
uses internally, reached through a small C shim so the C++ plugin code
never has to include libxo's non-C++-safe internal headers), so their
understanding of field syntax never drifts from the library's own.
.Pp
These plugins are entirely optional.
Code built without them runs exactly as it always has, parsing format
strings at run time.
Neither plugin changes
.Nm libxo Ns 's
public behavior or output;
.Nm xo_precompile
specifically only ever changes
.Em when
a format string is parsed, never
.Em what
it means.
.Sh BUILDING THE PLUGINS
The plugins require LLVM/clang development files (specifically
.Ic llvm\-config
and the matching Clang cmake package) and
.Ic cmake .
They are built as a
.Ic cmake
subproject, driven from the normal autoconf/automake build via
.Fl \-with\-llvm\-config :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&../configure \-\-with\-llvm\-config=/opt/local/libexec/llvm\-19/bin/llvm\-config \e
    \-\-prefix ~/work/root
.Ed
.Pp
If
.Fl \-with\-llvm\-config
is omitted (or
.Ic llvm\-config
can't be found),
.Ic configure
disables the plugins entirely and the rest of the build is unaffected.
.Pp
On macOS, the plugins are built with the same clang that provides
.Ic llvm\-config ,
which is not Apple's system clang and does not know where the macOS
SDK headers live.
.Ic configure
locates the SDK automatically via
.Ic xcrun ;
override it with
.Fl \-with\-sdk\-path=PATH ,
or suppress the
.Fl isysroot
flag entirely with
.Fl \-with\-sdk\-path=no :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&../configure \-\-with\-llvm\-config=/opt/local/libexec/llvm\-19/bin/llvm\-config \e
    \-\-with\-sdk\-path=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX.sdk
.Ed
.Pp
After configuring, build normally; the plugins build alongside the
rest of the tree and install into
.Pa ${prefix}/lib :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cd build
make CFLAGS='\-O2'
make install
.Ed
.Pp
Building just the plugins (skipping the rest of
.Nm libxo )
can be done from their subdirectory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cd build/llvm\-plugins
make
.Ed
.Pp
This produces
.Pa validate/xo_validate.so
and
.Pa precompile/xo_precompile.so .
Both are
.Ic cmake Ns \-driven
subprojects;
.Ic configure
seeds each one's cmake cache with the right
.Ev LLVM_DIR ,
.Ev Clang_DIR ,
target architecture, and install prefix, so plain
.Ic make
in that directory is sufficient \(en there is no separate
.Ic cmake configure
step to run by hand.
.Sh THE "xo_validate" PLUGIN
.Nm xo_validate
is a standard clang
.Fl fplugin
AST plugin.
It runs alongside the compiler's normal parsing and codegen (it does
not replace or suppress them), inspecting every call to
.Fn xo_emit
and its variants
.Po
.Fn xo_emit_h ,
.Fn xo_emit_hf ,
.Fn xo_emit_hvf ,
the
.Sq _p
wrappers, etc.
.Pc
whose format string is a compile-time string constant.
For each one it checks:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Sy Syntax
\(en malformed field descriptors, such as an unclosed
.Sq { .
.It
.Sy Argument count
\(en too few or too many arguments for the fields in the format
string.
.It
.Sy Argument type
\(en the type each field's display format expects (following the same
length-modifier rules as
.Fn printf ,
e.g.
.Li %ld
expects
.Vt long ,
.Li %zu
expects
.Vt size_t )
is compared against the actual argument's type, after the usual
varargs promotions.
.It
.Sy Style
\(en a number of
.Ic xolint Ns \-style
warnings about field naming conventions (upper case, underscores,
leading digits, names that are too short, anchors with conflicting
width sources, and so on).
.El
.Pp
Diagnostics are reported through clang's normal diagnostic engine, so
they appear exactly like any other compiler warning, with source
location and a caret pointing at the offending call:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xo_validate_demo.c:22:13: warning: libxo: format expects 2 argument(s) but 1 provided
   22 |     xo_emit("{:name/%s} {:age/%d}\en", "bob");
      |             ^
.Ed
.Pp
By default these diagnostics are warnings, not errors, so a build with
the plugin loaded does not fail because of them (unless the
surrounding build otherwise treats warnings as errors, e.g.
.Fl Werror ) .
This is a deliberate choice: syntax/type problems in a format string
are almost always real bugs worth fixing, but a checker with corner
case false positives shouldn't be able to break an otherwise-correct
build.
The
.Fl mllvm Fl xo\-validate\-errors\-as\-warnings
flag exists but is a no-op today since diagnostics are already
warnings; it is present for future use if a hard-error mode is added.
.Ss Invoking xo_validate
Invoke the validate plugin using
.Fl fplugin= ,
pointing at the built
.Pa .so :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clang \e
    \-fplugin=/path/to/xo_validate.so \e
    \-I/path/to/xo/include \e
    \-isysroot $(xcrun \-\-show\-sdk\-path) \e
    myprogram.c
.Ed
.Pp
It can also be loaded during a normal compile (the diagnostics appear
alongside the compiler's own, and compilation proceeds as usual):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clang \-c \e
    \-fplugin=/path/to/xo_validate.so \e
    \-I/path/to/xo/include \e
    myprogram.c \-o myprogram.o
.Ed
.Pp
To integrate it into a real build, add the two
.Li \-fplugin=... / \-I...
flags to
.Ev CFLAGS
for the translation units to be checked; there is nothing else to
configure or link against.
.Pp
Since it only inspects the AST and does not need to link, using the
.Fl fsyntax\-only
option is the fastest way to run it standalone, if only syntax
checking is needed.
.Sh THE "xo_precompile" PLUGIN
.Nm xo_precompile
is an LLVM
.Em "new pass-manager module"
plugin, loaded using
.Fl fpass\-plugin= .
It runs early in the optimization pipeline
.Pq Li PipelineStart ,
before inlining or constant-folding can obscure a call or the string
it references, and looks for calls to
.Fn xo_emit
and its variants where the format-string argument resolves to a
constant C string.
.Pp
For each such call it will:
.Bl -enum
.It
parse the format string with
.Nm libxo Ns 's
own parser (via the shared C shim), producing the same offset-based
field table
.Pq Vt xo_field_info_t Ns []
and pre-parsed display-format table
.Pq Vt xo_fspec_t Ns []
that
.Fn xo_emit
would otherwise build at run time, on every call.
.It
emit those tables as
.Li private constant
LLVM globals in the module.
.It
rewrite the call to the matching
.Li xo_emit_cached*()
entry point
.Po
.Fn xo_emit
becomes
.Fn xo_emit_cached ,
.Fn xo_emit_hf
becomes
.Fn xo_emit_cached_hf ,
and so on
.Pc ,
passing a pointer to the generated table ahead of the format string
and value arguments.
.El
.Pp
At run time,
.Fn xo_emit_cached
skips straight to formatting using the supplied table instead of
first scanning the format string to build one.
Output is byte-for-byte identical to what
.Fn xo_emit
would have produced from the same format string and arguments \(en the
pass changes only when the format string gets parsed, never the
result.
.Pp
If precompiling isn't possible or safe, calls are left untouched,
falling back to ordinary
.Fn xo_emit
and parsing format strings at run time.
This is needed when:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the format string isn't a compile-time constant (e.g. it was built at
run time, or came from a variable),
.It
the shim reports a parse error for it (the ordinary runtime path will
report the same error, so nothing is silently swallowed), or
.It
the callee isn't one of the known
.Fn xo_emit
names, or is called indirectly through a function pointer.
.El
.Pp
Because the rewrite happens at the IR level, it works regardless of
what source-level macros or wrappers were used to reach
.Fn xo_emit ,
as long as the call and its format-string argument survive to IR in a
form the pass can see.
It does
.Em not
require any source changes \(en the same
.Pa .c
file compiles correctly whether or not the pass is loaded, just faster
when it is.
.Ss Invoking xo_precompile
Invoke the precompile module by passing the
.Fl fpass\-plugin=
option on a normal compile; nothing else about the command line
changes, and the resulting object file links against the same
.Nm libxo
as an unmodified build
.Po
.Fn xo_emit_cached
and friends are ordinary exported
.Nm libxo
symbols, not something the plugin invents
.Pc :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clang \-c \e
    \-fpass\-plugin=/path/to/xo_precompile.so \e
    \-I/path/to/xo/include \e
    myprogram.c \-o myprogram.o

clang myprogram.o \-L/path/to/xo/lib \-lxo \-o myprogram
.Ed
.Pp
To see the effect directly, dump LLVM IR instead of an object file and
look for the generated
.Li .xo_fields.* / .xo_fspecs.* / .xo_fcache.*
globals and the
.Fn xo_emit_cached
call:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clang \-S \-emit\-llvm \e
    \-fpass\-plugin=/path/to/xo_precompile.so \e
    \-I/path/to/xo/include \e
    myprogram.c \-o \- | grep \-A2 xo_fcache
.Ed
.Pp
.Nm xo_validate
and
.Nm xo_precompile
are independent but can be used together \(en one checks correctness
at the AST level, the other rewrites at the IR level \(en so both
flags are normally passed on the same command line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clang \-c \e
    \-fplugin=/path/to/xo_validate.so \e
    \-fpass\-plugin=/path/to/xo_precompile.so \e
    \-I/path/to/xo/include \e
    myprogram.c \-o myprogram.o
.Ed
.Ss Wiring into a build
For a project already building against
.Nm libxo ,
add the plugin flags to
.Ev CFLAGS :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make CFLAGS='\-O2 \-fplugin=/path/to/xo_validate.so \-fpass\-plugin=/path/to/xo_precompile.so'
.Ed
.Pp
For a project that would rather not edit its own build files, the
.Ic xocc
wrapper script installed alongside
.Nm libxo
invokes the real compiler with both plugins already wired in, adding
the
.Fl L
and
.Fl lxo
flags automatically at the link stage.
It requires a verb
.Pq Cm full , Cm validate , "or" Cm precompile
as its first argument:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make CC='xocc full'
.Ed
.Pp
See
.Xr xocc 1
for the full set of verbs and invocation options.
.Sh CAVEATS AND KNOWN LIMITATIONS
.Bl -bullet
.It
Only calls with a
.Sy literal, compile-time-constant
format string can be precompiled.
A format string built with
.Fn sprintf ,
concatenation of a runtime value, gettext lookups that select between
strings at runtime, etc. is left as an ordinary
.Fn xo_emit
call.
.It
.Nm xo_precompile
currently registers at
.Li PipelineStart .
At
.Fl O0
this still fires under the current New Pass Manager, but if a future
LLVM version changes when
.Li PipelineStart
callbacks run at
.Fl O0 ,
building the precompiled translation units at
.Fl O1
or higher is the fallback.
.It
Both plugins are LLVM/clang-specific; there is no equivalent for GCC.
Code is fully portable either way \(en without the plugins it just
behaves like ordinary, unmodified
.Nm libxo .
.It
The plugins are versioned against
.Vt xo_field_info_t Ns 's
in-memory layout via
.Dv XO_EMIT_CACHE_VERSION
(in
.In xo.h )
and a set of
.Li _Static_assert
layout checks in the shared C shim
.Pq Pa llvm\-plugins/validate/xo_parse_shim.c .
A
.Nm libxo
built with a mismatched header will refuse the precompiled table at
run time and fall back to normal parsing rather than misinterpreting
it.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libxo 3 ,
.Xr xo_emit 3 ,
.Xr xo_options 7
